      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}\\), we start by simplifying the right-hand side. Notice that the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) can be factored. We perform polynomial division or factor by grouping:\n\n\\[x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8) = x(x-4)(x+2).\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[5 = \\frac{x(x-4)(x+2)}{x+2}.\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\neq -2\\), we can cancel the \\(x+2\\) terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[5 = x(x-4).\\]\n\nThis simplifies to the cubic equation:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0.\\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic equation as follows:\n\n\\[x^2 - 4x - 5 = (x-5)(x+1) = 0.\\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1.\\]\n\nWe must also check that these solutions do not make the original denominator zero. The denominator \\(x+2\\) is zero when \\(x = -2\\). Since neither \\(x = 5\\) nor \\(x = -1\\) is equal to \\(-2\\), both solutions are valid.\n\nThe sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is:\n\n\\[5 + (-1) = 4.\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{4}.\\]",
