      "text": "1. **Equation Simplification:**\n   To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\), we first need to simplify the right-hand side of the equation.\n\n   \\[\n   5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Polynomial Division:**\n   We perform polynomial division of \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\) by \\(x + 2\\).\n\n   - **First Step:**\n     Divide the leading term of the dividend \\(x^3\\) by the leading term of the divisor \\(x\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{x^3}{x} = x^2\n     \\]\n     Multiply \\(x^2\\) by \\(x + 2\\):\n     \\[\n     x^2 \\cdot (x + 2) = x^3 + 2x^2\n     \\]\n     Subtract this from the original polynomial:\n     \\[\n     (x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x) - (x^3 + 2x^2) = -4x^2 - 8x\n     \\]\n\n   - **Second Step:**\n     Divide the new leading term \\(-4x^2\\) by \\(x\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{-4x^2}{x} = -4x\n     \\]\n     Multiply \\(-4x\\) by \\(x + 2\\):\n     \\[\n     -4x \\cdot (x + 2) = -4x^2 - 8x\n     \\]\n     Subtract this from the current polynomial:\n     \\[\n     (-4x^2 - 8x) - (-4x^2 - 8x) = 0\n     \\]\n\n   - **Third Step:**\n     Divide the new leading term \\(0\\) by \\(x\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{0}{x} = 0\n     \\]\n     Multiply \\(0\\) by \\(x + 2\\):\n     \\[\n     0 \\cdot (x + 2) = 0\n     \\]\n     Subtract this from the current polynomial:\n     \\[\n     0 - 0 = 0\n     \\]\n\n   Thus, the quotient is \\(x^2 - 4x\\) and the remainder is \\(0\\). Therefore, we can write:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2} = x^2 - 4x\n   \\]\n\n3. **Setting the Simplified Equation:**\n   Substitute the simplified form back into the original equation:\n   \\[\n   5 = x^2 - 4x\n   \\]\n\n4. **Solving the Quadratic Equation:**\n   Rearrange the equation to standard form:\n   \\[\n   x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   Solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -4\\), and \\(c = -5\\):\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{-(-4) \\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-5)}}{2 \\cdot 1}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 20}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 \\pm 6}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   This gives us two solutions:\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 + 6}{2} = 5\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{4 - 6}{2} = -1\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sum of the \\(x\\)-values:**\n   The \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation are \\(5\\) and \\(-1\\). Their sum is:\n   \\[\n   5 + (-1) = 4\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
