      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2}\\), we start by simplifying the right-hand side. We can factor the numerator \\(x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x\\):\n\n\\[\nx^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8)\n\\]\n\nNext, we factor the quadratic expression \\(x^2 - 2x - 8\\):\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 2x - 8 = (x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\]\n\nThus, the numerator can be written as:\n\n\\[\nx(x - 4)(x + 2)\n\\]\n\nNow, the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(x \\neq -2\\), we can cancel the \\(x + 2\\) terms in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\n5 = x(x - 4)\n\\]\n\nExpanding and rearranging the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n5 = x^2 - 4x\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 4x - 5 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis is a quadratic equation, which we can solve using the quadratic formula \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\), where \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = -4\\), and \\(c = -5\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 + 20}}{2} = \\frac{4 \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2} = \\frac{4 \\pm 6}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{4 + 6}{2} = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{4 - 6}{2} = -1\n\\]\n\nThus, the solutions to the equation are \\(x = 5\\) and \\(x = -1\\). The sum of these solutions is:\n\n\\[\n5 + (-1) = 4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the \\(x\\)-values that satisfy the equation is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
