      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the values of \\( x \\) that satisfy the equation \\( 5 = \\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x + 2} \\). First, we start by simplifying the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nThe numerator of the fraction can be factored using polynomial division or by finding a common factor. We can rewrite the numerator as follows:\n\n\\[ x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x = x(x^2 - 2x - 8) = x(x - 4)(x + 2). \\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ 5 = \\frac{x(x - 4)(x + 2)}{x + 2}. \\]\n\nFor \\( x \\neq -2 \\), we can cancel the \\( x + 2 \\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[ 5 = x(x - 4). \\]\n\nThis simplifies to the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0. \\]\n\nWe can factor this quadratic equation as follows:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 4x - 5 = (x - 5)(x + 1) = 0. \\]\n\nSetting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:\n\n\\[ x - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x + 1 = 0, \\]\n\\[ x = 5 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -1. \\]\n\nThus, the solutions to the equation are \\( x = 5 \\) and \\( x = -1 \\). The sum of these solutions is:\n\n\\[ 5 + (-1) = 4. \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the \\( x \\)-values that satisfy the equation is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
